/*
ONE-HOUR TV DRAMA TEMPLATE (single-camera) — Fountain format.
Standard structure: Teaser/Cold Open + 4-5 Acts, ~45-65 pages total.
Act breaks are commercial breaks in broadcast; streaming dramas often keep the act
structure for pacing even without literal ad breaks.
See ../formatting/screenplay-format-spec.md for TV-specific format notes.
*/

> COLD OPEN <

INT./EXT. << LOCATION >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

<< A hook: a mystery, a cliffhanger, or a scene that sells this episode's premise
before the titles roll. >>

> END OF COLD OPEN <

> MAIN TITLES <

# ACT ONE
= Establish this episode's A, B, and (if any) C stories. Plant the act-out hook.

INT./EXT. << LOCATION >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

<< Scene. >>

> END OF ACT ONE <

# ACT TWO
= Complications. The A story's plan meets its first real obstacle.

INT./EXT. << LOCATION >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

<< Scene. >>

> END OF ACT TWO <

# ACT THREE
= Midpoint reversal for this episode. Subplots start colliding.

INT./EXT. << LOCATION >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

<< Scene. >>

> END OF ACT THREE <

# ACT FOUR
= The episode's low point / escalation toward the act-out.

INT./EXT. << LOCATION >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

<< Scene. >>

> END OF ACT FOUR <

# ACT FIVE
= Climax and resolution of this episode's arcs; plant the hook for next episode.

INT./EXT. << LOCATION >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

<< Scene. >>

> END OF SHOW <

/*
SERIES-BIBLE NOTE: keep a running document of persistent world/character state across
episodes — see ../../04-worldbuilding/ and ../../05-character/. Track each episode's
A/B/C story against the season's serialized arc so standalone episodes still pay off
the larger Save the Cat / Story Circle arc for the season.
*/
